WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.468
-David, it's so lovely
to see you.

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:03.870
-Hi, Seth Meyers.
Good to be here.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.638
-Hi.
-You're on a real set.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.274
You're on your set, unless you
remodeled your house.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.476
-No, this is the real set now.
Yeah.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:13.646
Yeah, week two of trying to
pretend like things are normal.

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:18.584
-Okay. An odd thing happened
to me just now.

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:22.221
I'm in my office,
and I realized that,

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:25.491
you're going to be on TV
in a few minutes.

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:27.560
So I went to -- I thought,
"Oh, my God,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.762
I better, like, spruce up."

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:33.032
I went to the bathroom
and sprayed my armpits with...

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.368
you know that air freshener
stuff?

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.470
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-That we keep in the bathroom.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.171
And I thought,
"Oh, that's good.

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:41.107
I'm going to smell okay
on television."

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.410
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.244
-We don't have that
technology yet,

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:47.780
but I would love to --
if we could get smell

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.417
through Zoom technology,
that would help.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.086
David, your show was
the last Broadway show

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:56.689
I saw before
everything shut down.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.858
And it is an incredibly
optimistic,

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:02.795
uplifting show that felt
very necessary for that time.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:04.630
It seems even more
necessary now.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:08.668
Is it a show that you hope to
get back on stage again?

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.669
-Yeah, yeah, we hope to get it
back on stage.

00:01:10.669 --> 00:01:13.072
I think it's gonna be --
realistically,

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.074
I think it's going to
be about a year.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:16.809
-Mm-hmm.
-I mean,

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:20.813
there's the vaccine
and there's how long

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:23.616
it takes the vaccine to
get out there,

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.753
and how long for people
to feel comfortable,

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:30.189
even with masks on,
sitting right next to strangers,

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:31.958
because, you know,
the Broadway thing is

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:36.328
you can't do Broadway theater
with 25% capacity.

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:38.731
-Of course
-It has to be like 80%.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.933
And so -- Yeah.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.502
That might take a while.
That's okay.

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:46.405
We've got this movie
coming in the meantime.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:50.175
-Now, so this is -- you
have this production filmed,

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:54.213
and you had it filmed by
none other than Spike Lee.

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:58.317
How does that collaboration
come about?

00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:01.553
-I texted Spike.
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:04.424
Yes.
Text is a wonderful thing.

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:06.792
So...

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.161
We'd crossed paths
a few times,

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.830
and so I had his text number,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.601
and I -- there was --

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:18.104
there were stirrings
that somebody might want to make

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:22.642
a movie of this show,
so I texted him and said,

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.410
"There's -- Somebody might
make a movie of this.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.512
Do you want to come see it?
Check this out."

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.781
So, he came to Boston.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.349
It was a two-show day.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:32.351
We do two shows on Saturdays.

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:34.854
And by the second show,

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:36.589
afterwards he came to
my dressing room and said,

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.557
"I want to do this."

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.894
So then it was like, okay,
get the money in place,

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:42.194
and all that kind of stuff.

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:44.530
It was kind of a scramble
to get it all together.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.133
And it worked.
I was thrilled,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:51.171
very, very, very happy
with the way it came out.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:52.538
-I was wondering,
for someone like you,

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.206
that obviously you're on stage.

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:55.909
It's not something that you --

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:59.011
I'm assuming in rehearsals
you step back and watch things.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:02.782
But are you someone who can
enjoy watching yourself?

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:05.284
Like, when you -- is it nice
to have this sort of

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:09.289
now timestamped moment of when
you were doing this show?

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:12.158
-I realized when I saw
the first cut

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:14.560
that Spike and the editor
showed me,

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.530
that it's the first time
I've seen the show.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.965
And I thought, "Oh, my God,
I've been doing this

00:03:19.965 --> 00:03:23.203
for a couple years now
and I had never seen it."

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:25.538
And of course, I'm looking
and going,

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:28.107
"Look what they're
doing back there.

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.109
Look what those other musicians
are doing back there.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.612
I had no idea they were
doing that."

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:38.151
But also, in general, though,

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:39.852
I'm a little older
than I used to be.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.621
I don't take as much pleasure
looking at myself

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:45.424
as I used to.

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:46.926
-Well, there are --
It should be noted,

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.393
there are a lot of other
musicians on stage

00:03:48.393 --> 00:03:49.762
for you to look at, so...

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.098
-Yes, there's very handsome

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:54.233
and beautiful musicians
on stage, yes.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.102
-I wonder, you know, you --

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.070
for my money and for a lot
of people's money,

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.939
you made the greatest
concert film of all time,

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.773
"Stop Making Sense,"
and I wonder

00:04:01.773 --> 00:04:03.575
if there was
anything from that

00:04:03.575 --> 00:04:05.811
that you brought into this

00:04:05.811 --> 00:04:08.280
insofar as what you wanted
to be grasped

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.748
from a live performance?

00:04:09.748 --> 00:04:11.250
Because this does have
such energy,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.753
the same sort of energy I felt
when I was in the audience.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:17.890
-Um, with
"Stop Making Sense" --

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.159
Oh, gosh. I know.
I know. I know. I know.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.961
It's a tough bar.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:25.798
Jonathan raised the bar pretty
far, and so did we, the band.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.801
But, I think we're up to it.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.104
I think we're up to it.

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:34.473
There was a moment during
the filming where Spike,

00:04:34.473 --> 00:04:36.141
who was good friends
with Jonathan,

00:04:36.141 --> 00:04:38.077
kind of looked up to the ceiling
and goes,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.812
"Jonathan, how we doing?
How we doing?

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.581
I think we're doing
pretty good here.

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.916
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.652
-That's fantastic.
We also talked --

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:50.223
you have a magazine,
"Reasons to be Cheerful."

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:53.125
And you're starting a new,
I guess...

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:55.060
maybe you can describe it
to us,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.762
the "We Are Not Divided,"

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:59.665
it's sort of a project
that is, again,

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:02.768
has this same sort of optimism,
that in this time that,

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:05.371
you know, were you to watch,
you know,

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.206
cable news most of the day,
you would think that

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:08.641
there's two halves
of this country.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:11.744
But you sort of have found
that maybe people are

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:15.081
more similar than
we're willing to let on.

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.450
-Yeah, so I have this kind of

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:22.254
solutions journalism...
website, magazine

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.256
that we look for, well,

00:05:24.256 --> 00:05:27.193
evidence of people who
have found solutions to things

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:30.162
that we think maybe
are intractable.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:34.200
And my colleague suggested
that, well, kind of

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.235
in the run-up to the election,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.404
that we do a series
called "We Are Not Divided,"

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:41.607
where we report on instances
where people are actually

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.176
bridging the gaps between
themselves

00:05:44.176 --> 00:05:47.079
and finding that we're not
maybe as divided

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:49.915
as we are
led to think we are.

00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.550
I know.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.153
So when they first
proposed this,

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:57.623
I responded and said,
"Are you kidding?"

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.893
You know, I mean,
I wake up in the morning,

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:02.561
I read the papers,
and I think we're all

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:04.230
ready to kill one another.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.533
And they said, "Can you show me
some examples?"

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.301
Which they did, which they did.

00:06:09.301 --> 00:06:10.737
And I said,
"This is really great.

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:12.672
So let's do this.
We're going to do this

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.006
for, I don't know
what it is,

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:17.409
like, six weeks or so
up until the election.

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:21.179
And hope that it will make
people realize

00:06:21.179 --> 00:06:25.551
that maybe we're not as
divided as we think we are.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:28.220
-I will leave it on that,
because as always,

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.957
I am in a better mood after
I see you than I was before.

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:34.827
That is true whether we talk
or whether I see your show.

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:36.729
And I'm so glad that those who
weren't lucky enough to see it

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:38.965
on stage can see it on HBO.

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:41.267
And it's just always
a delight, David. Thank you.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.569
-Thank you very much.
Good to see you again.

